fn fix_table() str {
	return "" +
		"E001\tstart the file with `package main` (or the package's name), and keep only one.\n" +
		"E002\tmove the import up, under the package declaration.\n" +
		"E003\tcheck the path (it is relative to the current directory) and that the file is readable.\n" +
		"E004\tcheck the pattern against the files (the directory is the one of the source file), or move the directive onto the `let` line above it.\n" +
		"E010\tremove the character, or put the text in a string.\n" +
		"E011\tcorrect the digits, or write the number in a base whose digits it uses.\n" +
		"E012\tuse one of the units (`30s`), or put a space or an operator between the number and the name.\n" +
		"E013\tclose the string; for text over several lines, use a raw backquote string or `\\n`.\n" +
		"E014\twrite one character, or use a string (`\"ab\"`) for more.\n" +
		"E015\tdouble the backslash (`\"C:\\\\path\"`), or use a raw backquote string, which has no escapes.\n" +
		"E020\tadd or remove what the message names. The cause is often just before the position: a missing comma, operator, parenthesis or brace.\n" +
		"E021\tgive the constant a value (`const height = 480`). (In edition 0, `const width, height = 640` also gives this code: one value per name.)\n" +
		"E030\tclose the value with `}}`, double a literal brace (`{{{{`, `}}}}`), or use a raw backquote string, which does not interpolate.\n" +
		"E040\tput the attribute on the function it is meant for, or remove it.\n" +
		"E041\tcorrect the spelling (`@json(\"id\")`), or remove the attribute.\n" +
		"E042\twrite the name as a string: `@json(\"id\")`.\n" +
		"E043\tlist the element types (`(i64, str)`), or leave the result out when there is none.\n" +
		"E044\tgive the last field names their type (`Rect(x f64, w, h f64)`), or list only types (`Rect(f64, f64, f64)`).\n" +
		"E045\tlist the variants (`type Shape enum {{ Circle(r f64), Empty }}`), or use a struct.\n" +
		"E046\tname the parameters (`fn first[T constraints.Any](xs []T) T`), or leave the brackets out.\n" +
		"E047\t`type Page[T constraints.Any] struct {{ ... }}` (and `import \"constraints\"`).\n" +
		"E050\tput the label on the loop it names, or remove it.\n" +
		"E051\tadd the arms to wait on, or remove the `select`.\n" +
		"E052\tbind one name. To number the elements of a slice, range over the slice: `for i, x in xs`. To count down, count up and compute the value: `for i in 0..n {{ let j = n - 1 - i }}`.\n" +
		"E053\tadd the arms, ending with `_ => ...` when the arms do not cover every value.\n" +
		"E054\tuse literal endpoints, or bind the value and test it in a guard.\n" +
		"E055\tdefer a call (`defer f.Close()`, `defer say.Line(\"done\")`); wrap anything else in a function and defer a call to it.\n" +
		"E090\twrite the edition 1 form the message names.\n" +
		"E091\tsplit the declaration: `let width = 640` and `let height = 480` on their own lines.\n" +
		"E101\trename one of the declarations, or merge them into one.\n" +
		"E102\tgive the declaration another name.\n" +
		"E103\tdeclare the name before using it, correct its spelling, or import its package.\n" +
		"E104\tuse the package's exported API (toolchain/docs/STDLIB.md); in your own package, capitalize the name to export it.\n" +
		"E110\tcorrect the path; for a package in the program's own tree, write it relative to the importing file (`import \"./geom\"`).\n" +
		"E111\treview the change; if it is intended, run `tin vendor` to copy the dependency again and record the new hashes (or regenerate the entry with `shasum -a 256 example.tin`).\n" +
		"E112\tadd the package to `tin.mod` with `require` and run `tin vendor`.\n" +
		"E113\timport only one of them; a vendored package should not reuse the name of a standard package or of another dependency.\n" +
		"E114\tcorrect the line in the package's source and run `tin vendor` again.\n" +
		"E115\trun `tin vendor`, and review the capability the dependency now asks for.\n" +
		"E120\tadd `fn main()` to the program's `package main`.\n" +
		"E121\tdeclare `fn main()` and read the command line with the `lever` package.\n" +
		"E130\tmove the declaration that is used above the one that uses it.\n" +
		"E131\tassign the global where every core runs: in its initializer, in an `on core.start` handler, or in a `once` block of code the cores run (`examples/percore.tin`).\n" +
		"E140\twrite a function that takes the value (`fn double(n i64) i64`), or declare a named type (`type Count i64`) and give it the method.\n" +
		"E201\tkey the map by a value that stands for the slice, such as a `str` built from it, or a struct of its fields.\n" +
		"E202\tgive the array a length of zero or more; for other elements, use a slice and `append`.\n" +
		"E203\tinitialize the variable (`mut p = Point{{}}`), use `?T` for a value that may be missing, or make the slice empty with a capacity (`make([]T, 0, n)`) and `append`. For a map whose keys may be missing, give it `?dyn S` values: `m[k]` then reads nil for a missing key.\n" +
		"E204\ttake the values as separate parameters (`fn sum(a i64, b i64)`), or a struct.\n" +
		"E205\thold the next values through a slice (`kids []Node`), or make the type a reference struct.\n" +
		"E210\tpass one argument per parameter; to take any number of values, declare the last parameter variadic (`xs ...T`) or pass a slice.\n" +
		"E211\tpass the slice itself (`first(xs)`), or put the spread slice last in a call to a variadic function.\n" +
		"E213\tcorrect the field name, or add the field to the struct.\n" +
		"E214\tcorrect the method name, or declare the method (`fn (u User) Save()`).\n" +
		"E215\tcall a function; to convert, write the type (`i64(x)`).\n" +
		"E216\tset the fields the struct declares, by name.\n" +
		"E217\tgive the builtin the arguments it takes (`delete(m, \"k\")`).\n" +
		"E220\twrite the value with literals and constants, or make it a variable.\n" +
		"E221\twrite a constant of the right kind (`const name = \"7\"`), or convert where it is used.\n" +
		"E222\tdivide by a constant that is not zero.\n" +
		"E223\tuse a wider type, or a value in range.\n" +
		"E224\tshift a wider type (`u16(x) << 8`), or a count below the width.\n" +
		"E230\tconvert explicitly (`i64(x)`, `str(b)`, `mint.Atoi(s)`), or give the variable the value's type.\n" +
		"E231\tparse text with `mint.Atoi(s)` (or `mint.ParseFloat`), and format a number with `say.Str(n)`.\n" +
		"E232\tcompare explicitly (`if n != 0`, `if s != \"\"`, `if p != nil`).\n" +
		"E233\twrite the type (`let x ?Point = nil`).\n" +
		"E234\tgive as many names as there are values (use `_` for the ones you do not need).\n" +
		"E235\tselect from the package (`say.Line`), or build a value of the type (`T{{}}`).\n" +
		"E236\tcompare to get a `bool` (`n != 0`), or convert the operand.\n" +
		"E237\tcompare the parts that have equality, or give the type a method that compares.\n" +
		"E238\tindex with an integer (convert with `mint.Atoi` or `i64(x)`).\n" +
		"E239\tpass every value of the variant (`Shape.Rect(2, 3)`), or use a variant the enum declares.\n" +
		"E240\tdeclare the fields that hold secrets `secret` (`pass secret str`) and keep the struct plain.\n" +
		"E241\tleave the secret out of the message, or write `reveal(x)` where showing it is intended (`tin audit secrets` lists every `reveal`).\n" +
		"E242\tdeclare the parameter or variable `secret`, or pass `reveal(x)` on purpose.\n" +
		"E243\tcompare with `seal.Equal(a, b)`, which takes the same time whatever the values hold; to order secrets, reveal them on purpose first.\n" +
		"E244\tkey the map by a hash of the secret, such as `seal.Sha256Hex(token)`.\n" +
		"E245\tdrop the `reveal` from a plain value; for a struct, reveal the secret field itself (`reveal(u.token)`).\n" +
		"E250\tbound only a `str`, a slice or a map; check a number's range with an `if`.\n" +
		"E251\tcheck the length with `bound`, which fails with `fault.LimitExceeded` when it does not fit: `u.name = try bound(raw)`.\n" +
		"E252\twrite the bounded type: `let b str max 100 = try bound(raw)`.\n" +
		"E260\tadd the `return` for the remaining paths.\n" +
		"E261\tuse `return` to leave the function, or move the statement into the loop.\n" +
		"E262\treturn every result, in order (`return 1, \"one\"`).\n" +
		"E263\trange over a slice, map or `str` field of the value, or give the type a method that returns one.\n" +
		"E264\tmove the statement before the one that leaves, or delete it; to leave only sometimes, put the leaving statement in an `if`.\n" +
		"E270\tmove the loop body into a function and `defer` there; defer a function that handles the fault rather than one that returns it.\n" +
		"E280\twrite the value in the literal: `run(\"SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = {{id}}\")`.\n" +
		"E281\tencode a struct of plain data (numbers, `str`, `bool`, slices, maps, structs and enums of those).\n" +
		"E282\tpass the format first (`say.Out(\"%d\\n\", n)`), or use `say.Line(n)`; give each verb a value of its type, or use `%v`.\n" +
		"E290\tname every value of the variant (`case Rect(w, h)`), and match only variants of the enum.\n" +
		"E291\tadd the missing cases, or a `default` arm when the rest share one answer.\n" +
		"E292\tremove the arm, move the catch-all `_` arm last, or put a constant before the range that holds it (`404 =>` before `400..500 =>`).\n" +
		"E293\tend each block arm with its value, or leave from it.\n" +
		"E295\twrite `(A, A)` where the results go, in each generic function that returns them.\n" +
		"E310\tstore `keep(x)` instead of `x`, or keep the value in a local that ends with the request.\n" +
		"E311\tstore `keep(x)`, or pass the closure only to functions that call it during the request.\n" +
		"E312\tpass a local instead of the long-lived value, or make the function store `keep(x)`.\n" +
		"E313\t`keep()` the value before the reset, or create it again after.\n" +
		"E314\t`let kept = keep(msg)` before the block and use `kept` in it, or write `keep(msg)` inside the block.\n" +
		"E315\tmake what must outlive the block its value (`last = arena {{ ... line }}`), grow outside slices and maps after the block from its value, or `keep()` the value (long-lived memory).\n" +
		"E316\treturn the data the caller needs (a name instead of a handler), and build the rest after the block.\n" +
		"E401\tgive the call its own statement (`let n = mint.Atoi(\"2\") catch _ {{ 0 }}`) and use the result.\n" +
		"E402\twrite `fn parse() !i64`, `return v` on success and `fail \"msg\"` on failure.\n" +
		"E403\tgive the call its own line (`let n = try mint.Atoi(s)`), then use `n`.\n" +
		"E410\tpass it upward with `try`, handle it with `catch`, or test the `err` you assign.\n" +
		"E411\tdeclare the result as `!i64` (and its callers check it), or handle the fault in place with `catch`.\n" +
		"E412\twrite `return 1`, and `fail err` where the function fails.\n" +
		"E413\tpass the fault upward with `try`: `try mint.Atoi(s) wrap \"reading the count\"`.\n" +
		"E414\tremove the `try` or `catch`.\n" +
		"E415\tend the block with the value to use (`0`), or leave from it.\n" +
		"E416\t`fail \"negative count\"`, or `fail say.Fault(\"bad count %d\", n)`.\n" +
		"E417\tdeclare `let ErrNotFound = fault(\"not found\")` and `fail ErrNotFound`, or use `fail \"not found\"` for a one-off fault.\n" +
		"E420\tuse the function or fault itself (`nil` is its missing value), or keep it in a struct and make the struct optional.\n" +
		"E421\tcheck it first: `if u != nil {{ return u.Name }}`.\n" +
		"E422\tuse the value as it is, or make it optional (`let n ?i64 = 3`) where it can be missing.\n" +
		"E501\tremove the type arguments (`p Point`), or give the declaration type parameters (`type Point[T constraints.Any] struct {{ x T }}`).\n" +
		"E502\tgive one type argument per parameter (`Pair[str, i64]`).\n" +
		"E503\trecurse with the same type arguments, or move the varying part into a value (a depth counter, a slice) instead of a type.\n" +
		"E504\twrite the type arguments (`Zero[i64]()`), or pass an argument whose type names them.\n" +
		"E505\tput a type in the brackets (`Zero[i64]()`), and pass values in the parentheses.\n" +
		"E506\twrite the parameters by name (`Stack[T]`), or make the method a generic function.\n" +
		"E510\tpass values of a listed type (convert them: `f64(n)`), or add the type to the union.\n" +
		"E511\tcompare a comparable key instead (an id, a `str`), or write a function that compares the elements.\n" +
		"E512\tadd the method with the signature the message gives, or pass a type that has it.\n" +
		"E513\tchange the method to the signature the message gives.\n" +
		"E514\tpass the concrete value, or convert the concrete value to the `dyn` shape you need.\n" +
		"E515\tname the parameter: `fn Send(w dyn io.Writer)`.\n" +
		"E516\twrap the value in a struct that has the shape's methods.\n" +
		"E520\twrite the shape as the constraint (`[W io.Writer]`), or drop the type parameter and take a `dyn io.Writer` value.\n" +
		"E521\tlist only concrete types in the union; to combine shapes, list them in a shape body (`shape ReadCloser {{ Reader; Closer }}`).\n" +
		"E522\tlist the methods you need (`Write(data []u8) !i64`), or a shape that declares them.\n" +
		"E523\tremove one of the listings, and declare the methods directly where they belong.\n" +
